Veyra milestone suggestions
===========================

Current milestone
-----------------
- Finish and independently verify the focused M4 interaction-loop correction pass in `milestone.md`.
- Do not expand M4 with unrelated editor UX work while its hit-test/pointer correctness contract is still under verification.

High-priority next milestone candidate — Workspace UX Stabilization
------------------------------------------------------------------
The editor is now technically capable enough that workspace usability is becoming the bottleneck. Before expanding into large new Rive feature families, stabilize the everyday authoring experience.

### 1. Canvas navigation / graph feel
- Rework zoom so it is smooth, predictable, and anchored to the pointer/cursor position where appropriate.
- Define min/max zoom and make repeated wheel/trackpad zoom steps feel consistent instead of jumping too quickly.
- Tune wheel/trackpad scroll and pan sensitivity independently from zoom sensitivity.
- Support natural middle-mouse pan and Space+drag pan without fighting selection/authoring tools.
- Preserve the current viewport center correctly through zoom, window resize, panel resize, and artboard resize.
- Add `Fit Artboard`, `Fit Selection`, `100%`, and `Focus Selection` commands.
- Prevent the user from effectively losing the scene after zooming/panning far away.
- Make navigation behavior deterministic enough to test without depending on browser accident.

### 2. Object focus / locate controls
Every persistent drawable hierarchy item should expose a focus/locate affordance.

Required behavior:
- hierarchy row has a focus/target icon;
- clicking it selects the object and moves the viewport to its evaluated bounds;
- choose a sensible zoom that keeps the whole object visible with padding;
- groups focus their evaluated child bounds rather than an invented rectangle;
- repeated focus is deterministic;
- focus works after arbitrary zoom/pan and after human renames;
- stable refs/IDs drive lookup, never the display name;
- expose the underlying focus operation cleanly enough that AI/runtime tools can request the same viewport action later.

### 3. Hierarchy visibility controls
- Keep/add an eye icon on every supported hierarchy row.
- Eye icon must clearly reflect visible/hidden state.
- Clicking it changes the canonical `visible` property through the normal mutation/command path where applicable.
- Parent visibility and child visibility remain distinct and understandable.
- Hidden rows stay discoverable in the hierarchy.
- Do not conflate visibility with interaction `pointerEvents` or opacity.

### 4. Resizable / collapsible workspace panels
The left hierarchy, right inspector, and bottom timeline must no longer have fixed-only dimensions.

Required behavior:
- draggable splitter between hierarchy and canvas;
- draggable splitter between canvas and inspector;
- draggable splitter above the timeline/bottom panel;
- sensible minimum/maximum sizes;
- collapse/expand buttons for left, right, and bottom panels;
- double-click or dedicated action may restore a useful default size;
- persist panel sizes/collapsed state locally between reloads;
- canvas/viewBox recalculates immediately after splitter changes;
- no lost object, broken hit-test mapping, or timeline clipping after panel resize;
- responsive fallback for small screens remains usable.

### 5. Object transform/resize authoring
- Audit current drag/move behavior for pointer-offset jumps and transform-space mistakes.
- Add dependable on-canvas resize handles for supported node geometry.
- Support corner and edge resize where meaningful.
- Modifier behavior should be explicit and consistent (for example aspect lock / center resize / snapping where later supported).
- Rotation, parent transforms, non-uniform scales, and pivots must not make resizing jump or invert unexpectedly.
- Group resize behavior must be explicitly defined rather than guessed.
- Authoring drag/resize transactions must use the existing Store begin/mutate/commit/cancel boundary and stay undoable.
- Pointer interaction preview/listeners must remain isolated from authoring gestures.

### 6. Theme system overhaul
The current theme selector changes only part of the palette and the magenta/pink default is visually inconsistent.

Replace ad-hoc color overrides with a complete semantic-token theme system covering the whole application:
- app/background;
- top bar;
- panels;
- canvas workspace;
- artboard frame;
- hierarchy rows;
- inspector;
- timeline;
- borders/dividers;
- buttons/tool states;
- text/muted text;
- selection;
- focus;
- controls/handles;
- warnings/errors/success;
- overlays/tooltips/modals if present.

Requirements:
- selecting a theme changes the complete application, not only internal accents;
- no hard-coded magenta/pink should remain outside the theme token definitions unless semantically intentional;
- provide a coherent neutral/dark default rather than the current mismatched pink-heavy palette;
- each theme must maintain readable contrast and distinguish selected/hover/focus states;
- preserve the selected theme locally;
- theme switching must not alter document content or exported artwork colors.

Suggested initial themes:
- Neutral Dark (recommended default)
- Graphite / Blue
- Deep Teal
- Warm Dark
- Light Neutral

### 7. Workspace visual cleanup
- Reduce visual noise and inconsistent spacing.
- Normalize icon sizes, button heights, panel headers, borders, and radii.
- Improve selected/hover/focus hierarchy states.
- Make canvas the visual center of the editor instead of the surrounding chrome competing with it.
- Keep color reserved for meaningful state/action instead of tinting every surface.
- Audit graph/timeline readability at different panel sizes and zoom levels.

### 8. UX regression suite
Add deterministic tests where practical for:
1. zoom anchored around cursor;
2. zoom/pan never produces NaN/invalid viewBox values;
3. `Fit Artboard` returns the full artboard to view;
4. focus-object finds the correct stable ref after extreme pan/zoom;
5. focus-object remains correct after rename/reorder;
6. left/right/bottom panel resize clamps to valid ranges;
7. panel collapse/expand restores previous size;
8. viewport/hit-test mapping remains correct after panel resize;
9. hierarchy eye control changes only visibility;
10. hidden object can still be focused/located from hierarchy (while remaining hidden unless user unhides it);
11. drag/resize produces one undoable authored transaction;
12. theme switch updates all semantic UI tokens but does not mutate the Veyra document;
13. all existing M0–M4 architecture/interaction tests remain green.

### Acceptance gate
Do not call this milestone complete until the editor can be used for an extended authoring session without fighting zoom, pan, panel sizing, object location, resizing, or theme inconsistencies.

